<h2 id="application">Application and AI</h2>
<dl class="glossary-grid"><dt>RAG</dt><dd>Retrieval-augmented generation: retrieve relevant reference text, then give it to a model as context for answering. Retrieval and generation are separate operations.</dd><dt>Chunk</dt><dd>A bounded piece of a manual. Its source ID, product, manual and chunk index remain attached so an answer can show evidence.</dd><dt>Embedding</dt><dd>A numeric representation used to compare text. This workshop uses 1536 values from one shared Azure embedding deployment.</dd><dt>Vector similarity</dt><dd>A way to rank embeddings by distance. The SQL operator <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> is cosine distance; smaller distance means a closer match.</dd><dt>Grounding</dt><dd>Using supplied evidence to support an answer. A citation lets the participant inspect the evidence; it does not guarantee the model's reasoning is correct.</dd><dt>Migration / Flyway</dt><dd>A versioned database schema change and the tool that applies/records it. Flyway is not an ORM and does not create your Azure database server.</dd><dt>Python</dt><dd>The language used for the short Function and worker. <code>def</code> defines a function, dictionaries hold named values, and <code>with</code> manages resource lifetimes such as connections.</dd></dl>
<h2 id="containers">Containers and Kubernetes</h2>
<dl class="glossary-grid"><dt>Docker image / container</dt><dd>An image packages files and startup configuration; a container is a running instance. A Dockerfile describes how to build an image.</dd><dt>Tag / digest</dt><dd>A tag is a convenient image name that can move. A digest identifies image content. Workshop tags are versioned from build inputs rather than called latest.</dd><dt>ACR</dt><dd>Azure Container Registry stores the built Products and ProductsChat images. Its pull permission belongs to the AKS kubelet identity.</dd><dt>AKS</dt><dd>Azure Kubernetes Service manages the Kubernetes control plane. The workshop creates a dedicated node pool; no pre-existing cluster is needed.</dd><dt>Pod / Deployment / Service</dt><dd>A Pod runs containers; a Deployment reconciles their desired number/version; a Service routes traffic to matching ready Pods.</dd><dt>Probe</dt><dd>A health check. Startup allows initialization; readiness controls traffic eligibility; liveness checks whether to restart a stuck process.</dd><dt>KEDA</dt><dd>A scaler that reads event-source metrics and changes Kubernetes replicas. It does not process queue messages or call the LLM.</dd><dt>HPA</dt><dd>Horizontal Pod Autoscaler: the Kubernetes controller KEDA supplies with metrics to scale between active replica counts.</dd></dl>
<h2 id="azure">Azure services and access</h2>
<dl class="glossary-grid"><dt>Blob container / blob</dt><dd>A logical grouping and an object in Azure Blob Storage. The private manuals container stores text blobs; it is unrelated to Docker containers.</dd><dt>Event Grid</dt><dd>Notification delivery that something happened, such as a blob being created. Its event identifies the blob; it does not carry the complete manual.</dd><dt>Service Bus</dt><dd>A broker for queued work and application communication. Requests and replies have separate queues with locks, acknowledgements, retries and dead-letter handling.</dd><dt>Azure Function / serverless</dt><dd>Code invoked by a configured trigger on platform-managed servers. Hosting dependencies, networking, identity and failure handling still exist.</dd><dt>Managed / workload identity</dt><dd>Managed identity gives an Azure resource an Entra identity. AKS workload identity exchanges a Kubernetes service-account token for that identity's Azure tokens through federation.</dd><dt>RBAC</dt><dd>Role-based access control: a principal, a role and a scope. Having a token proves identity; a suitable role grants the operation.</dd><dt>Key Vault / App Configuration</dt><dd>Key Vault stores actual secrets. App Configuration stores ordinary settings. Both are read at startup in this implementation; no live refresh is implied.</dd><dt>Private DNS / NAT gateway</dt><dd>Private DNS resolves PostgreSQL to its VNet address. NAT supplies explicit outbound internet connectivity for the AKS and Function subnets.</dd></dl>
<h2 id="delivery">Delivery and consistency</h2>
<dl class="glossary-grid"><dt>Request ID / correlation ID</dt><dd>Persistent UUIDs identifying a question and its request/reply relationship. They appear in the database, messages, logs and polling URL.</dd><dt>Outbox</dt><dd>An outgoing message saved in the same database transaction as its request, then sent separately. It prevents a crash between committing a question and trying to send it from losing the work.</dd><dt>Idempotency</dt><dd>Handling repeated work without unwanted duplicate effects. Chunk uniqueness, immutable checksums, per-key locks, cached worker results and guarded reply updates support it here.</dd><dt>Message lock / acknowledgement</dt><dd>A temporary claim to process a message, followed by completion after success. Renewal handles long model calls; it does not promise exactly-once delivery.</dd><dt>Dead-letter queue / poison queue</dt><dd>Places to inspect work that cannot be processed after delivery failures. Event Grid, Service Bus and the Function Blob extension have separate failure stores.</dd></dl>
